course: dictionary
lesson: 4945
title: Impressive and the big claim
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Impressive is an adjective. It describes something so good, big, skilful or beautiful that people admire it. Teachers and stories use it as praise: «an impressive drawing», «impressive mountains».
explain: Use the clues around the word. In «an impressive score», the word score tells you it is about a result, and the word impressive tells you the result is so good that people notice it. Check by swapping in admired, very good, or worth praising, and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Impressive comes from the verb impress, which means to make someone admire you. Be careful with a boast: «A boast is a big claim about yourself, your family or your things, made to sound impressive.» A boast is usually hyperbole, an exaggeration, not a careful lie.
fact: Impressive means so good, big, skilful or beautiful that people admire it.
fact: «An impressive drawing» is one that people stop to admire.
fact: In maths and science, «an impressive 95 per cent» means a very high result, close to perfect.
fact: Impress is the related verb: to make someone admire you.
fact: A boast is a big claim made to sound impressive; it is usually hyperbole, not a careful lie.
exercise: «The mountains were impressive, with snow on their tops.» What does impressive mean here? | Very dark at night | Cold and wet all year round | *So big and beautiful that people admire them
exercise: «An impressive 95 per cent» in a test tells you what about the score? | *It was a very high, admirable score | It was a poor score | It was a score nobody counted | It was 5 per cent wrong
exercise: Which word is the related verb of impressive? | impressive | impression | *impress | impressively
test: Zara says, «My drawing of the horse was impressive.» What does impressive mean? | So small that nobody sees it | So old that it is broken | *So good that people admire it | So wet that it drips
test: «The boast was impressive, but it was not true.» What is a boast? | A quiet whisper in a library | *A big claim about yourself, made to sound impressive | A careful, true report | A kind of map
exam: Which word could best replace impressive in «an impressive score»? | Sad | Hidden | Tiny | *Admirable
